---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const reactInstallSnippet = `npm install @kernelui-lib/react @kernelui-lib/styles`;

const reactImportSnippet = `import "@kernelui-lib/styles";
import "@kernelui-lib/react/styles.css";

import { Button } from "@kernelui-lib/react";`;

const nextServerSnippet = `// app/settings/page.tsx: a Server Component, no "use client" needed
import { Button, TextField } from "@kernelui-lib/react";

export default function SettingsPage() {
  return (
    <form action="/api/settings" method="post">
      <TextField label="Display name" name="displayName" />
      <Button type="submit" variant="primary">Save</Button>
    </form>
  );
}`;

const elementsInstallSnippet = `npm install @kernelui-lib/elements @kernelui-lib/styles`;

const elementsImportSnippet = `<script type="module">
  import "@kernelui-lib/elements";
</script>

<kernel-button variant="primary">Save changes</kernel-button>`;

const tokensOnlySnippet = `npm install @kernelui-lib/styles`;

const shadcnAddSnippet = `npx shadcn@latest add https://www.kernelui.com/r/dialog.json`;

const shadcnNamespaceSnippet = `// components.json
{
  "registries": {
    "@kernel": "https://www.kernelui.com/r/{name}.json"
  }
}`;

const shadcnNamespaceAddSnippet = `npx shadcn@latest add @kernel/dialog @kernel/text-field`;

const shadcnResultSnippet = `/* src/index.css: added once, by the kernel-ui item */
@import "@kernelui-lib/styles" layer(base);
@import "@kernelui-lib/react/styles.css";

// src/components/kernel/dialog.tsx
export { Dialog } from "@kernelui-lib/react";
export type { DialogProps, DialogState, DialogClassNames, DialogBackdrop, DialogLabels } from "@kernelui-lib/react";`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Installation" description="Install @kernelui-lib/react, @kernelui-lib/elements, or @kernelui-lib/styles and import the token and component stylesheets." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Installation</h1>
    <p>
      Every package reads its colours, radius, spacing, and type from{" "}
      <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code> — install it alongside whichever
      component package you're using, it's a dependency either way, listing
      it explicitly just keeps its version visible in your own{" "}
      <code>package.json</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>CLI (recommended for React)</h2>
    <p>
      The fastest way to wire up a React project — especially when migrating
      from shadcn/ui:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={`npx @kernelui-lib/cli init`} lang="bash" />
    <p>
      See the <a href="/cli/">CLI guide</a> for <code>kernel doctor</code>,
      <code>kernel docs</code>, and options like <code>--tailwind</code> and{" "}
      <code>--accent</code>. Migrating from shadcn/Radix? Read the{" "}
      <a href="/migration/">migration guide</a> after init.
    </p>

    <h2>React</h2>
    <p>
      The flagship package: every component in the library, as real React
      components over real semantic HTML. Requires React and ReactDOM 18 or
      newer as peer dependencies.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={reactInstallSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>Import the tokens once, then the component stylesheet, then start using components:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={reactImportSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>
      Skip the <code>@kernelui-lib/react/styles.css</code> import and every
      component still renders as its real underlying element, fully
      functional, just with no Kernel-specific look — see{" "}
      <a href="/platforms/">platforms</a> for more on headless usage.
    </p>

    <h3>Next.js App Router and Server Components</h3>
    <p>
      The package's bundle starts with a <code>"use client"</code>
      directive, so you can import Kernel straight into a Server Component
      file (a <code>page.tsx</code> or <code>layout.tsx</code> with no
      directive of its own) and pass it props. You don't need a wrapper
      Client Component. The components themselves still render and run on
      the client: Kernel is interactive, so every component is a Client
      Component, and its props must be serialisable, the same rule as any
      Client Component. Event handlers like <code>onValueChange</code> belong
      in a component you mark <code>"use client"</code> yourself, and so do
      calls to the package's plain functions (<code>toast()</code>,{" "}
      <code>linesFromCode()</code>, <code>mergeRefs()</code> and the other
      helpers), because the whole bundle is client code.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={nextServerSnippet} lang="tsx" />

    <h3>Letting your CSS win</h3>
    <p>
      To override Kernel from your own stylesheet without specificity
      fights, import <code>@kernelui-lib/react/styles.layer.css</code> in
      place of <code>styles.css</code>. It puts every component rule in a
      cascade layer, so any CSS you write outside a layer wins. See{" "}
      <a href="/theming/#overriding-component-styles">theming</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="shadcn">With the shadcn CLI</h3>
    <p>
      Kernel publishes a <a href="/r/registry.json">shadcn registry</a>, so a
      project already set up for shadcn/ui can add Kernel components with the
      shadcn CLI, and the shadcn MCP server can browse them. Every component
      page's slug is its item name:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={shadcnAddSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>Or register Kernel as a namespace once and use short names:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={shadcnNamespaceSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <CodeBlock code={shadcnNamespaceAddSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>
      Unlike a shadcn/ui component, a Kernel item doesn't copy the component's
      source into your project. Kernel is an npm package, and its components
      keep updating with it. Adding an item does three things:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>installs <code>@kernelui-lib/react</code> and <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code>;</li>
      <li>
        adds the two stylesheet imports to your CSS file, the first time only.
        The tokens and reset go into Tailwind's <code>base</code> layer, so
        Tailwind's preflight, your own base styles and every utility still take
        precedence;
      </li>
      <li>
        writes <code>components/kernel/&lt;name&gt;.tsx</code>, a re-export of
        the component and its types, so you can import from
        <code>@/components/kernel/dialog</code> like any other shadcn
        component. It won't overwrite your shadcn
        <code>components/ui/</code> files.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <CodeBlock code={shadcnResultSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>
      Don't add <code>@kernelui-lib/styles/tailwind.css</code> to a shadcn
      project. It maps Tailwind theme names such as <code>border</code> and
      <code>accent</code> to Kernel's tokens, which would restyle your shadcn
      components. To theme Kernel, set <code>--kernel-*</code> tokens as
      described in <a href="/theming/">theming</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Web Components</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code> ships the same design system as
      framework-agnostic Custom Elements, for Vue, Svelte, Astro, or plain
      HTML.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={elementsInstallSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>Import the tokens and the elements' own stylesheet in your CSS, then the elements module in a script tag:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={`@import "@kernelui-lib/styles";
@import "@kernelui-lib/elements/styles.css";`} lang="css" />
    <CodeBlock code={elementsImportSnippet} lang="html" />
    <p>
      See <a href="/platforms/">platforms</a> for framework-specific notes —
      Vue's <code>isCustomElement</code> option, Svelte, Astro.
    </p>

    <h2>Tokens only</h2>
    <p>
      Building your own components on top of Kernel's scale, without any of
      its component opinions? Install just the tokens:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={tokensOnlySnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>
      See <a href="/theming/">theming</a> for the full token reference.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
